# 12) Best practical production tweak: strip code fences before parse

Even with strict instructions, models sometimes wrap JSON in ```json fences.

You can add a repair helper.

Create `stripJsonFences.ts` or place inline in `parse.ts`:

```ts
export function stripCodeFences(raw: string): string {
  return raw
    .replace(/^```json\s*/i, "")
    .replace(/^```\s*/i, "")
    .replace(/\s*```$/, "")
    .trim();
}
```

Then in `service.ts`:

```ts
import { stripCodeFences } from "./stripJsonFences";

const responseText = await callYourModel({
  system: SYSTEM_PROMPT,
  user: buildUserPrompt(options),
});

const cleaned = stripCodeFences(responseText);
const result = safeParseBrainDumpPlan(cleaned);

if (result.success) {
  return result.data;
}

try {
  const parsed = JSON.parse(cleaned);
  const normalized = normalizeBrainDumpPlan(parsed);
  return BrainDumpPlanSchema.parse(normalized);
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Plan parse failed:", result.error);
  console.error("Raw model output:", responseText);
  throw new Error(
    error instanceof Error ? error.message : "Failed to parse plan response"
  );
}
```

This is a genuinely useful resilience upgrade.

